# The wire protocol

> The HTTP contract underneath the SDK

The SDK exposes money tasks through `run()` and structured records through
`forUser(id).data`. Read this page when porting to another runtime or inspecting
the requests the SDK sends. Schemas are in the API reference tab.

Four HTTP calls and one browser redirect: the token exchange, interact (blocking or streaming, one call on two routes), the approvals poll, and user-action. Consent is a navigation, never an API call. Two optional GETs sit beside them: `/connect/tool-definition` (unauthenticated, static) and `/connect/apps/me` (the app self-read).

Two base URLs appear below. Server requests go to Railway; consent and approval pages live on the console. Set `PAYMAN_BASE_URL` to the Railway origin when using the SDK, especially older builds that default to the unavailable custom hostname. The SDK discovers the console origin from your app registration.

| Name | What it is |
| --- | --- |
| `PAYGENT_CLOUD_URL` | The API. Every server-to-server call. Default `https://paygent-cloud-server-production.up.railway.app`. |
| `CONSOLE_URL` | Your customer's Payman console, where they consent and approve. |

| Header | Carries |
| --- | --- |
| `x-paygent-connect-app-key` | Your app key. Every call. |
| `x-paygent-connect-grant` | The grant token. Every call after the exchange. |
| `x-paygent-environment` | `sandbox` or `live`, on the interact and stream responses; the stream's only environment signal. |

Confidential clients send the first two together. Public OAuth clients send
their token in `x-paygent-connect-grant` without an app key; omitting a key does
not make a confidential grant public. Hosted `/mcp` uses `Authorization: Bearer`
instead of these REST headers.

## The consent redirect

When your app offers Plaid and multi-provider wallets are enabled, customers can
add several banks inside one Plaid session. They approve each bank with its own
sign-in, then finish the session. Payman saves each bank separately and includes
all of those bank connections in the app's approved wallet. Plaid's optional phone
sign-in is separate from signing in to Payman or approving access at a bank.

Build the link on your server and navigate top-level. No iframe, no popup: the customer is signing in to Payman. The SDK's popup consent depends on the SDK-served callback page; hand-rolled flows keep the plain navigation.

```js
import { randomBytes } from "node:crypto";

// CSPRNG, stored against this signed-in user, single use, 10-minute TTL.
const state = randomBytes(32).toString("base64url");
await saveState(user.id, state, { ttlMs: 10 * 60 * 1000 });

// Hold the question they asked, so you can answer it when they return.
await savePendingQuestion(user.id, message);

const authorize = new URL("/connect/authorize", CONSOLE_URL);
authorize.searchParams.set("app_id", APP_ID);
authorize.searchParams.set("redirect_uri", REDIRECT_URI); // exactly as registered
authorize.searchParams.set("state", state);

```

`scopes` defaults to `interact`. Request `financial_data:read` explicitly for
structured financial records, or use the comma-separated
`interact,financial_data:read` value when both permissions are needed. OAuth
authorization uses the singular `scope` parameter with space-separated values.
Existing grants need new consent before they can use additional permissions.

